│ StatsController) -> Service/CodeGenerator layer -> Spring Data JPA           │
│ UrlMappingRepository -> H2 embedded database, now configured in file-based   │
│ mode (jdbc:h2:file:./data/urlshortener or similar path under a configurable  │
│ data directory) instead of jdbc:h2:mem:, so both URL mappings and click      │
│ counters (already columns on the JPA-managed UrlMapping entity, not a        │
│ separate HashMap) survive JVM restarts via H2's on-disk .mv.db file.         │
│ Cross-cutting error handling is centralized in GlobalExceptionHandler        │
│ (@RestControllerAdvice), which intercepts exceptions thrown by               │
│ controllers/services (HttpMessageNotReadableException for malformed JSON,    │
│ CodeNotFoundException for missing codes, InvalidUrlException for validation, │
│ and a new catch-all Exception handler) and converts them into a structured   │
│ ErrorResponse record containing timestamp, status, error, message, and path, │
│ serialized as JSON with the correct HTTP status. Because the rate limiter    │
│ filter runs before dispatch and writes its own response body directly, it is │
│ structurally isolated from this advice and its 429 behavior is untouched.    │
│ Observability is extended by leaning entirely on Spring Boot Actuator's      │
│ auto-configured DataSourceHealthIndicator, which Spring already wires up     │
│ because a DataSource bean is present; no custom HealthIndicator class is     │
│ needed \u2014 only application.properties tuning                             │
│ (management.endpoint.health.show-details, exposure include) to ensure the    │
│ 'db' component is visible in /actuator/health responses. All changes are     │
│ additive/config-level, preserving existing endpoint paths, request/response  │
│ schemas, and validation rules.",                                             │
